  "reasons": [
    "Standard repeated observations drifted: download -19.14%, upload -32.28%, warm first-byte +16.96%.",
    "Aggressive versus nearby standard-final offered only +3.12% download and +5.11% upload, while warm first-byte was 5.88% slower. These observations do not establish a benefit.",
    "Conservative upload was below both standard observations and did not demonstrate a compensating latency gain.",
    "Initial standard had one fresh-client timeout and conservative had two; aggregate measurement_valid is false. Their successful warm and payload phases remain bounded observations, not a clean full-trial comparison.",
    "All successful response CF-Ray regions were SJC, but resolved destination addresses were not pinned. Region consistency does not remove target or route drift.",
    "Idle observations do not show a return to cold-request latency; retain existing keepalive and timeout."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Tests were sequential and sample counts are small; no reliable causal winner or p95 is established.",
    "Time-interpolated standard is descriptive and cannot remove nonlinear time drift.",
    "Measured transfer rates include HTTP/TLS overhead over short bounded payloads; these are not long-term bandwidth guarantees.",
    "Active transfer overlap is verified; continuous saturation of the physical link is not proven.",
    "Fresh requests used select-only isolated clients, not production AUTO with startup healthchecks.",
    "Account-wide online and journal evidence does not uniquely identify one isolated QUIC connection.",
    "Mihomo client and HY2 server profiles both changed per trial; the results evaluate each paired profile, not either sender independently."
  ]
